<div class="container">

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 1: OVERVIEW ═══════════ -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been awarded at 93 ceremonies spanning from the 6th Academy Awards
    in 1934 through the 98th ceremony in March 2026. This dataset consolidates every winner into a single canonical record,
    tracing the evolution of film music's highest honor across nine decades.
  </p>
  <p>
    The award category has undergone several name changes and structural shifts across its history. In certain years, the Academy
    split the category into separate awards for dramatic scores and comedy/musical scores; this dataset presents one
    consolidated winner per ceremony year. The 93 winning scores represent <strong>{kpi_total_unique} distinct composers</strong>
    (counting individuals within shared awards) and <strong>{len(set(r['film'] for r in rows))} unique films</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The composer pool reveals a striking concentration: <strong>{kpi_repeat_rate}%</strong> of all ceremonies were won by a composer
    who had already claimed the award at least once before. A handful of names appear again and again across the decades,
    while many composers appear just once — a testament to both the towering dominance of certain figures and the category's
    capacity to recognize new voices.
  </p>
  <p>
    The data spans from the Golden Age of Hollywood studio orchestras through the synthesizer experiments of the 1980s,
    the orchestral renaissance of the 1990s and 2000s, and into today's eclectic landscape where traditional orchestral
    writing coexists with electronic, experimental, and globally influenced scores.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: TOP COMPOSERS ═══════════ -->
