    the interplay of generational turnover among composers, Academy voting dynamics, and broader industry
    shifts in how films are scored and produced.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Era Transitions Mirror Industry Transformation</h3>
  <p>
    The Golden Age studio system fostered a stable roster of contract composers who won repeatedly.
    The Silver Age opened the door to composers from television, jazz, and pop. The Modern Era saw
    blockbuster orchestral scores coexist with synthesizer experimentation. The Contemporary Era,
    from 2000 onward, has embraced an increasingly global and stylistically diverse field — electronic,
    world music, minimalist, and genre-blending approaches that would have been unthinkable
    in the category's early decades. This widening aperture is arguably the most significant
    long-term trend in the data.
  </p>

  <h3>4. The Repeat-Winner Effect Is Real but Not Deterministic</h3>
